// The wall-clock slice of upstream `wasi:clocks@0.2.0`, vendored.
//
// This file exists so that the `Clock` capability can be granted as the *real*
// WASI interface rather than a bespoke look-alike — the demonstrator's related-work
// argument is that WASI interfaces are one instance of capability-as-interface, and
// granting `wasi:clocks/wall-clock` directly is the honest demonstration: a node
// whose `with` clause names `Clock` imports this interface, and a node whose clause
// does not carries no clock import at all.
//
// Only `wall-clock` is vendored. The upstream package also defines
// `monotonic-clock`, which depends on `wasi:io/poll` and would drag a second
// package in for an interface nothing here grants. What matters for import
// identity is the fully-qualified name — `wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0` — which is
// spelled here exactly as upstream spells it, so a component built against this
// file is indistinguishable at the boundary from one built against the full
// upstream package.

package wasi:clocks@0.2.0;

/// WASI Wall Clock is a clock API intended to let users query the current time.
/// A wall clock is a clock which measures the date and time according to some
/// external reference. It is not necessarily monotonic.
interface wall-clock {
    /// A time and date in seconds plus nanoseconds.
    record datetime {
        seconds: u64,
        nanoseconds: u32,
    }

    /// Read the current value of the clock.
    now: func() -> datetime;

    /// Query the resolution of the clock.
    resolution: func() -> datetime;
}
